mariupol february the 27th three days  after russia's war on ukraine has begun  an air evacuation is underway  taking injured ukrainians to safety  the pictures were filmed by paramedic  yulia payevska with a small camera on  her forehead she would continue filming  in the beleaguered city until her  capture by the russians  julia is a star athlete in ukraine as  well as a paramedic she'd been given the  headcam to film for a documentary about  the invictus games  instead she captured intimate and moving  testimony of life inside the besieged  city of mariopol as the russian assault  takes hold  paramedics struggle to keep up with more  and more casualties  in the most harrowing moments julia  films as a brother and sister arrive  injured from a shooting at a checkpoint  their parents are already dead  where does it hurt sunshine she asks the  girl she reassures the child while  others try to resuscitate her brother in  vain  [Music]  i hate that she says crying her face to  the wall  [Music]  in the darkness outside she tells a  colleague the boy has gone  maybe the girl will survive  of course she says they are prisoners of  war  the russians have not been merciful in  their assault on mario pole unsparing in  their utter destruction of the city in  its wake they have rounded up and taken  away many ukrainians  among them yulia pyesca seized after  these pictures were taken  the last footage she films with her  camera  two weeks of war in besieged mario pole  she says  soon afterwards she and her friend were  abducted by the russians their  whereabouts are unknown  but not before she was able to smuggle  out her footage  a moving and sometimes harrowing record  of the courage of paramedics on the  front line saving lives in their  devastated city dominic waghorn sky news  hakeef
 

